+# Decision: Cache Eviction Policy for Preview Worker
+
+## Context
+
+The preview worker maintains a 12 GiB cache of rendered contact sheets. Editors work in two distinct patterns: long sequential screenings that produce many one-use sheets, and comparison sessions that repeatedly access a small working set of clips. A single eviction policy must handle both without degrading either workload. The policy must remain internal to the worker and invisible to the API contract — all three candidate policies support the same `GET /internal/previews/{clip-id}/{revision}` interface with 200/202/404 outcomes.
+
+## Decision Elements
+
+**Gates (inherited):**
+- API contract unchanged: all candidate policies support 200 (cached), 202 (pending), and 404 (unknown) responses.
+- Cache remains within 12 GiB JPEG data limit.
+- No external visibility: cache files, eviction metadata, and pins are private to the preview worker.
+
+**Weighted criteria:**
+
+- **Protection against sequential scan disruption** (major): Long screenings introduce many one-use sheets that crowd out reused clips. The eviction policy should resist evicting heavily-accessed sheets during a big screening.
+- **Operational simplicity** (major): Bookkeeping overhead directly impacts the render job latency for cache hits. Simpler counters reduce per-hit cost.
+- **Adaptation speed to editor switches** (moderate): When one editor's session ends and another begins, the cache should shift from serving stale clips to the new working set quickly, without being locked into yesterday's favorites.
+- **Retention of repeatedly popular clips** (moderate): Clips accessed across multiple sessions should remain cached longer than one-off screenings.
+
+## Options in the Domain
+
+### Option 1: LRU (Least Recently Used)
+
+A simple recency-ordered queue. Every cache hit moves the sheet to the front; when admission would exceed 12 GiB, evict from the tail. Track only one timestamp or sequence number per entry.
+
+**Analysis:**
+- Protection against sequential scan: **weak**. A long sequential screening will move recently-accessed one-use sheets to the front, pushing out earlier comparison clips toward the tail. A full screening of 200 clips evicts 100+ cached entries.
+- Operational simplicity: **strong**. One timestamp per entry, one queue pointer update per hit. Negligible latency impact.
+- Adaptation speed: **strong**. Cache forgets old sessions immediately as new work streams to the front. Editors switching projects see fast adaptation.
+- Retention of repeated access: **weak**. A clip hit once yesterday but hit five times this morning ranks below a clip hit once five minutes ago.
+
+**Summary:** Minimal bookkeeping, fast adaptation, vulnerable to scan disruption.
+
+### Option 2: Frequency-Based Eviction with Aging
+
+Track access count per entry. On cache hit, increment a counter. Periodically age counters down (e.g., half-life every hour) so old popularity fades. On eviction, remove the entry with the lowest aged-frequency score.
+
+**Analysis:**
+- Protection against sequential scan: **strong**. A clip hit 50 times last week scores higher than a clip hit once during today's screening, even if both are recent. Scans do not disrupt popularity tiers.
+- Operational simplicity: **weak**. Requires counter maintenance per hit, periodic aging jobs (background thread or per-request amortization), and careful tuning of the aging schedule. A slow aging schedule keeps yesterday's favorites too long; fast aging degrades to near-LRU.
+- Adaptation speed: **partial**. Aging helps, but a frequently-accessed clip from the prior session is slow to forget. A clip hit 100 times last week may take hours to decay below a new clip's score.
+- Retention of repeated access: **strong**. Clips accessed across multiple sessions accumulate high scores and stay cached through occasional single-hit clips.
+
+**Summary:** Excellent scan protection and reuse retention, but aging tuning is a persistent operational burden and adaptation lags.
+
+### Option 3: 2Q (Two-Queue eviction)
+
+Two queues with a split of the cache space. On first cache hit, entries go to a small **in queue** (e.g., 10% of 12 GiB). On second hit to an in-queue entry, promote to a larger **out queue** (the remaining 90%). Eviction pulls from in queue first; only when in is exhausted, evict from out. Provides adaptation (one-hit entries flush quickly from the small queue) while protecting reused sheets (two-hit entries move to the protective out queue).
+
+**Analysis:**
+- Protection against sequential scan: **strong**. A long screening populates the in queue with one-use sheets. When a second hit arrives for a reused clip (from an earlier session or a comparison), it promotes to out. Scans cannot evict entries already in out, even if they are "older" by recency.
+- Operational simplicity: **strong**. Two queue pointers, one promotion threshold (second hit), no counters or periodic jobs. Hit cost is the same as LRU for in-queue entries; promotion is a pointer move for out-queue entries. Negligible latency addition.
+- Adaptation speed: **strong**. The small in queue (10% of budget) fills quickly with new work and flushes one-use sheets. Editors switching sessions see new clips enter the in queue immediately.
+- Retention of repeated access: **strong**. Any clip hit twice is promoted to out and protected from single-hit scans. Editors can repeatedly access a comparison set without loss during a big screening.
+
+**Summary:** Combines scan protection and reuse retention with the bookkeeping simplicity of LRU. Requires tuning the queue-size split but no ongoing aging maintenance.
+
+## Selection
+
+**Chosen: 2Q.**
+
+2Q trades a single tunable parameter (the queue-size split, initially 10/90) against **both** of the major criteria: it protects comparison sheets during big screenings (matching frequency-based protection) and requires only the bookkeeping simplicity of LRU (avoiding frequency's ongoing operational burden). The moderate criteria also align — adaptation is as fast as LRU, and reuse retention is as strong as frequency. The 10/90 split is a reasonable starting point; the in queue will typically hold 1.2 GiB of one-use sheets from a recent screening, leaving 10.8 GiB for reused clips. If editors report excessive eviction of reused clips, the split can be tuned without changing the fundamental mechanism.
+
+LRU was eliminated on the major criteria: it leaves comparison sheets vulnerable during screening (a significant gap for editors who rely on those clips). Frequency-based was eliminated on operational cost: the aging tuning is a persistent burden for a moderate improvement in adaptation speed that 2Q already provides through its small in queue.
+
+This decision engages the HLD tenet *Keep cache paths and replacement bookkeeping private to the preview worker* — the 2Q state (two queue heads and one split parameter) remains entirely within the worker and invisible to the API contract, satisfying the privacy constraint while meeting the workload goals.
+
+The mechanism commits the code to storing two queue pointers per cache entry and accepting that the queue-size split may be tuned in operation. Future work may parameterize the split via configuration if multiple preview workers with different workload mixes are deployed, but the 12 GiB total cache budget and the initial split are fixed for the pilot.
