+Cached JPEG data may occupy at most 12 GiB. Use a 2Q (two-queue) eviction policy to protect reused sheets during sequential screenings while allowing fast adaptation to new workloads.
+
+The 2Q policy maintains two in-memory queues, each ordered by recency. New cache hits enter the **in queue**, which holds at most 10% of the cache budget (1.2 GiB). When a sheet in the in queue is accessed a second time, it is promoted to the **out queue**, which holds the remaining 90% (10.8 GiB). On eviction, remove entries from the in queue first; only when the in queue is exhausted, evict from the out queue.
+
+This mechanism protects sheets accessed multiple times (comparison clips) from being evicted by a long sequential screening (which populates the small in queue with one-use sheets). Adaptation to new workloads is fast because the small in queue fills and flushes quickly. Before admitting a completed sheet, evict enough existing sheets (from the appropriate queue) to remain within the 12 GiB bound. Temporary render output is kept separately and removed on completion or failure. Eviction never deletes source footage or changes job-ticket semantics.
+
+For detailed rationale and analysis of eviction policy options, see `decisions/eviction-policy.md`.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|----------|--------|------------------------|-----------|
+| Cache eviction policy | 2Q (two-queue) | LRU, frequency-based with aging | 2Q protects reused clips from one-off screening scans while maintaining operational simplicity (no counter maintenance). See `decisions/eviction-policy.md` for full analysis. |
+| In-queue cache budget | 10% of 12 GiB (1.2 GiB) | 5%, 15%, 20% | 10% is a reasonable starting point based on typical screening patterns. The pilot will measure effectiveness; tuning is straightforward if data shows skew toward one-use or repeated access. |
+| Concurrent render jobs | 4 | 2, 6, 8 | Four jobs balance latency (overlapping renders hide I/O wait) against resource contention on the eight-core worker. Measured during pilot; can be tuned independently of eviction policy. |
+
+## Open Questions & Future Decisions
 
-The eviction policy is still open. Candidates are LRU, frequency-based eviction with aging, and 2Q. LRU has little bookkeeping and follows recent work quickly but is vulnerable to sequential scans. Aged frequency counts protect repeatedly popular clips but add counter maintenance and can lag a project switch. 2Q separates first-use entries from reused entries, resisting scans at the cost of extra queues and a queue-size parameter. None changes the external interface, queue concurrency, or disk budget.
+### Deferred
+1. Queue-size split tuning — data from the pilot may show the 10/90 split favors one-use or reused sheets disproportionately, requiring adjustment.
+2. Multiple preview workers — distributed cache coordination and cross-worker eviction policy. Out of scope for the pilot; requires synchronization beyond the current design.
